  "query_preview": "..\nToday through tomorrow: Another warm and dry fall day is ahead of us \ndue to the upper-level ridge over the western and central US \ncontinuing to dominate the weather pattern. High temperatures today \nwill be in the mid to high 70s, around 10 degrees above normal for \nthis time of year. Overnight lows will be closer to normal due to \nclear skies and light winds promoting efficient radiational cooling. \nNorthern sheltered valleys will see temperatures in the high 30s, \nwhile temperatures in the Columbia Basin, Spokane, and Palouse areas \nwill see lows in the mid 40s. High temperatures will warm by a \ndegree or two each day through tomorrow as the ridge restrengthens. \nEnjoy these warmer fall days while they last, because a cooldown is \nin order for the weekend. \n\nFriday through Sunday: Cluster analysis shows roughly 80 percent of \nensemble members favoring a strong trough moving through the area \nFriday afternoon. There is still uncertainty in the track, timing, \nand strength of the trough. This trough will usher in a cold front \nFriday afternoon, and as it does, a tight east-west surface pressure \ngradient will form across the state, resulting in gusty westerly to \nsouthwesterly winds. At this point, these winds appear to be the \nprimary impact. On Friday afternoon, wind gusts in the Waterville \nPlateau and Columbia Basin are forecast to increase to 30 to 35 mph, \nwith gusts 20 to 25 mph elsewhere. Given the prolonged stretch of \nlittle to no precipitation, blowing dust will be possible across the \nColumbia Basin where soils remain dry. These gusts will create \ndifficult travel conditions, especially for high profile vehicles, \nand could blow around unsecured objects.  \n\nThis cold front will also result in a sharp drop in temperatures \nfrom Friday to Saturday. Highs will drop 10 or more degrees, with \ntemperatures returning to seasonal normals in the upper 50s to low \n60s. It will also bring a return of precipitation chances, but \nmostly to the mountain areas. On Friday and Saturday, precipitation \nwill be confined to the Cascades and ID Panhandle mountains. \nSaturday night into Sunday, precipitation chances will increase \nacross the Okanogan Valley, northern mountains, and northern Idaho \nPanhandle as the upper low shifts southward and precipitation wraps \naround the system. Precipitation chances remain highest in the \nmountains, though snow levels do fall to around 4500-5000 feet \nSaturday night into Sunday, bringing a small chance for mountain \nsnow. Hikers and campers at these elevations and higher should be \nprepared for light snow and freezing temperatures. Elsewhere, \nprecipitation chances reach 15 to 20 percent. \n\nGusty conditions will continue Saturday and Sunday as winds from the \nnorth increase. On Saturday, gusty winds will be mostly confined to \nthe Okanogan Valley, Waterville Plateau, and basin areas. On Sunday, \ngusty winds will be more widespread, again reaching 30 to 35 mph in \nthe basin and Waterville Plateau. On Sunday, the chance for blowing \ndust in the Columbia Basin returns. Bottom line, expect a chilly and \nwindy weekend in the Inland Northwest, and stay tuned as the \nforecast becomes clearer in the coming days. /AS",
